philosophy was as sure at this point as at another. it was only, she
frequently said, those who expected to find amusement in themselves who
wandered about disconsolate and bored. amusement was to be derived from
watching others, when one permitted them to be entirely themselves.
one was born with oneself and, if one were intelligent, one got to know
oneself thoroughly at the age of four. thereafter, a life of boredom
intervened until the grave yawned, unless one surrounded oneself
with people who were individual enough to comport themselves with
